## ABSTRACT
Cationic azole-based metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with remarkable stability and unique pore environment have aroused great research interests. Meanwhile, flexible MOFs which can undergo pore-structure changes upon exposure to external stimuli are ideal materials for gas separation. However, introducing flexibility into the framework of cationic azole-based MOFs is rarely reported. Herein, we synthesized two stable isomorphic cationic MOFs (M-btz-as, \(\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{C o}\), Ni) based on a linear azole ligand. After activated at high temperature under vacuum, M-btz-ht \((\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{C o}\), Ni) were obtained and both MOFs exhibited flexible features in which Co-btz is more flexible than Ni-btz. Different solvent-mediated activation methods were employed to explore their effects on structural flexibility and produced MOFs with different phases. Continuous phase transformation of Co-btz-e was verified by powder X-ray diffraction. In addition, these MOF phases possessed different gas separation abilities affected by their flexible frameworks, and Co-btz-ht exhibited the highest \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) separation ability.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), cationic MOFs, flexibility, gas adsorption, carbon dioxide
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline porous materials constructed from metal centers and organic linkers [1]. Owing to their permanently porous frameworks and rich architectural diversity, MOFs have been widely developed in numerous applications such as gas sorption and separation, catalysis and sensors [2–6]. MOFs constructed with nitrogen containing linkers, such as pyrazoles, pyridines, 1,2,3-triazolate and 1,2,4-triazolate, etc., tend to exhibit better stability than those built with carboxylate bridging ligands [7–11]. Among them, the neutral nitrogen-containing ligands can produce cationic MOFs with positively charged frameworks which are balanced by anions in the pores [12–15]. Due to the unique pore environment (the ions in the pore can exchange with other external ions and build up specific interactions with the guests), cationic MOFs are effectively used in a variety of applications with excellent stability [13, 16–18]. S. Ghosh et al. [17] prepared a water stable cationic MOF (iMOF-1C), which exhibited simultaneous capture of toxic anions of \(\mathrm{SeO}_{4}{}^{2-}\) and \(\mathrm{HAsO}_{4}{}^{2-}\) with the highest record in MOFs field. Therefore, developing cationic azole-based MOFs will bring great benefits for MOFs' practical applications satisfying industrial requirements.
As the third-generation functional MOFs materials, flexible MOFs [19–23] can respond to external stimuli such as pressure [24], temperature [25], gas molecules [26–28] and solvents [29, 30]. Among the fascinating properties of flexible MOFs, so-called breathing effects represent the framework flexibility as a function of host–guest interactions [29, 31, 32],
As the third-generation functional MOFs materials, flexible MOFs [19–23] can respond to external stimuli such as pressure [24], temperature [25], gas molecules [26–28] and solvents [29, 30]. Among the fascinating properties of flexible MOFs, so-called breathing effects represent the framework flexibility as a function of host–guest interactions [29, 31, 32], and are corresponding to the expanded or compressed transformations of one-dimensional (1D) pores in MOFs between narrow pore (np) and large pore (lp) or closed pore (cp) and open pore (op) [21, 27, 32]. Even though the development of flexible MOFs with breathing effects is in its infancy, this kind of MOFs exhibits great application prospects for gas adsorption and separation [24, 29, 33]. For example, Long group has prepared flexible MOF \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{bdp})\) \((\mathrm{bdp}^{2-}=1,4\)-benzenedipyrazolate) with outstanding \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity because it can selectively adsorb \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) but complete exclusion \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) within a specific pressure range [33]. P. Llewellyn et al. demonstrated flexible MIL-53 under mechanical constraint possessed molecular sieving separation mechanism towards gases with similar sizes \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) [24]. Combination of reversible flexibility with cationic frameworks would create significant perspectives for gas separation in industry. However, it is a great challenge to introduce flexibility into the highly stable cationic MOFs considering that flexibility refers to structural transformations in MOF frameworks [34].
Herein, a linear neutral triazole ligand (1,4-bis(4H-1,2,4-triazol-4-yl)benzene, btz) was selected and two isomorphic cationic azole-based MOFs (M-btz-as, \(\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{C o}\), Ni) were synthesized which featured high stability with 1D rhomboidal channels. Direct desolvation at high temperature of these two MOFs produced two structures with pore volumes different from the original ones indicating their flexible frameworks. The reversible flexibility as well as influence of different activation methods on the flexibility of framework was discussed. Owing to the different opening degrees of these frameworks, different \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) versus \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivities were obtained.
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Materials and methods
2.1 Materials and methodsbtz was purchased from Nanjing mole medical science and technology Co., Ltd. All other reagents and solvents were purchased from local commercial suppliers and used without further purification. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data were collected using a Rigaku SmartLab X-ray diffractometer with \( \mathrm{Cu} \, \mathrm{K}\alpha \) radiation \( (\lambda = 1.5418 \, \text{\AA}) \). Elemental analyses were recorded by a Perkin-Elmer 2400 element analyzer. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was performed with a Mettler-Toledo TGA/DSC \( 3+ \) analyzer at a heating rate of \( 10 \, ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min} \) in an air atmosphere.
## 2.2 Synthesis of [Co(btz)Cl]Cl·2.5DMSO·4.5HzO (Co-btz-as) and [Ni(btz)Cl]Cl·4DMSO·2H2O (Ni-btz-as)
2.2 Synthesis of [Co(btz)Cl]Cl·2.5DMSO·4.5HzO (Co-btz-as) and [Ni(btz)Cl]Cl·4DMSO·2H2O (Ni-btz-as)Co-btz-as was synthesized with the procedure as below: \(0.1\;\mathrm{mmol}\) of \(\mathrm{Co(Cl)}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) \(0.04\;\mathrm{mmol}\) of btz, \(2~\mathrm{mL}\) of dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) and \(8~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) were added to a \(20~\mathrm{mL}\) glass vial in sequence, and the vial was placed in an air oven at \(120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . After cooling to room temperature, the obtained blue block-shaped crystals were collected and washed with DMSO, then dried in air. Yield: \(82\%\) . Elemental analysis \((\%)\) for Co-btz-as \(\mathrm{(C_{30}H_{4}C o N i_{12}C l_{5}S_{5}O_{14})}\) : Calculated: C, 30.00; N, 14.00; H, 5.33; Found: C, 29.56; N, 13.83; H, 4.54. Light purple crystals of Ni-btz-as were synthesized via a similar procedure with metal source of \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{Cl})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) and the yield was \(60\%\) . Elemental analysis \((\%)\) for Ni-btz-as \(\mathrm{(C_{18}H_{32}N i N_{6}C l_{12}S_{4}O_{8})}\) : Calculated: C, 31.30; N, 12.17; H, 5.22; Found: C, 31.30; N, 11.07; H, 5.67.
2.3 Preparation of Co-btz-x and Ni-btz-x \(\mathbf{\nabla}(\mathbf{x}=-\mathbf{b}\mathbf{t},\mathbf{\nabla}\mathbf{m}\) and -e)
2.3 Preparation of Co-btz-x and Ni-btz-x \( \mathbf{\nabla}(\mathbf{x}=-\mathbf{b}\mathbf{t},\mathbf{\nabla}\mathbf{m}\) and -e)The preparation methods of these two series materials are similar, and here Co-btz-x is taken to describe in detail. The heated phase of Co-btz (denoted as Co-btz-ht) was obtained by the activation under vacuum at \( 180 \, ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 24 \, \mathrm{h} \). Typically, \( 100 \, \mathrm{mg} \) Co-btz-as were soaked in \( 100 \, \mathrm{mL} \) solvents (methanol or ethanol) for 5 times in 2 days to exchange out DMSO guests thoroughly. The methanol-soaked samples and ethanol-soaked samples were named as Co-btz-m and Co-btz-e, respectively. Activated Co-btz-m and activated Co-btz-e were obtained by vacuum activation of the solvent-exchanged samples at \( 80 \, ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for 24 and \( 4 \, \mathrm{h} \), respectively.
