## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Structural analysis of Co-btz-as and Ni-btz-as
3 Results and discussion3.1 Structural analysis of Co-btz-as and Ni-btz-asSCXRD analysis revealed that Co-btz-as and Ni-btz-as were isostructural with different metal cores. Both of them crystallize in the orthorhombic crystal system with the Imma space group. Here, Co-btz-as was taken to present and discuss the structure in detail. In the asymmetric unit of Co-btz-as, there are one fourth of \( \mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II}) \), one fourth of \( \mathrm{Cl} \) and one fourth of the ligand in the host framework, as well as one fourth of \( \mathrm{Cl} \) as guest counter ion. As shown in Fig. 1(a), the metal ions \( \mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II}) \) display a distorted octahedron geometry and are six-coordinated with four \( \mathrm{N} \) atoms belonging to four individual btz ligands and two \( \mu_{2}-\mathrm{Cl}^{-} \)anions. The \( \mathrm{Cl}^{-} \)ions bridge metal centers to form 1D zigzag \( \left[\left(\mathrm{MCl}\right)_{n}\right]^{n+} \) inorganic chains along \( b \) axis and four \( \mathrm{N} \) atoms from different btz ligands connect with each metal to assemble the chains into three-dimensional (3D) open frameworks. The \( \mathrm{M}-\mathrm{Cl} \) and \( \mathrm{M}-\mathrm{N} \) distances in Co-btz-as are 2.4679 (11) and 2.123 (3) \( \text{\AA} \), respectively, longer than those in Ni-btz-as \( \left(\mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{Cl}: 2.4305(14) \, \text{\AA}, \mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N} 2.077(3) \, \text{\AA}\right) \), which may induce weaker bond strength and less rigidity of Co-btz-as. Both M-btz-as frameworks feature 3D rhomboidal channels in the \( b \) direction (Fig. 1(b)), which resemble many well-studied flexible MOFs, such as MIL-53 [36], MFM-133 [32],
NU-1400 [27], containing 1D channels with diamond cross-sections and exhibiting "breathing" behavior in a manner analogous to that of wine rack. The solvent-accessible volumes estimated using the PLATON program are \(1,164\,\mathrm{\AA}^{3}\) \((47.5\%)\) and \(1,113\,\mathrm{\AA}^{3}\) \((46.6\%)\) for Co-btz-as and Ni-btz-as, which are occupied by highly disordered DMSO and water guest molecules. The chemical formulas of Co-btz-as and Ni-btz-as were determined as \(\left[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{btz})\mathrm{Cl}\right]\mathrm{Cl}\cdot2.5\mathrm{DMSO}\cdot4.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\left[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{btz})\mathrm{Cl}\right]\mathrm{Cl}\cdot4\mathrm{DMSO}\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) based on the SCXRD analysis and elemental analysis.
## 3.2 Flexibility analysis of Co-btz and Ni-btz
In order to evaluate the flexible possibility of M-btz \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}\), Ni), diverse phases were prepared via different methods. PXRD analysis was employed to confirm the phase purity of bulk samples and demonstrate dynamic changes within the frameworks. Upon desolvation by heating at \(180\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\), Co-btz-ht and Ni-btz-ht were prepared as the fully activated phases proved by the TGA results (Fig. S1 in the ESM). As shown in Fig. 2, all the characteristic peaks for Co-btz-ht and Ni-btz-ht in the PXRD pattern were shifted towards higher \(2\theta\) angles compared to the pristine MOFs, demonstrating the occurrence of phase transformation. Based on the calculation from the Bragg peaks (002), the length of \(c\) axis of Co-btz reduced from 15.75 to \(13.88\,\mathrm{\AA}\) ( \(11.9\%\) decrease), while that of Ni-btz only encountered \(5.4\%\) decrease (Fig. 1(b)). This result indicated that Co-btz possessed more flexible framework than Ni-btz as expected.
Connolly surfaces of the 1D channels in M-btz-as and M-btz-ht were simulated with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) probe molecule via Materials Studio [25, 37] to visually display the pore changes caused by framework flexibility. Along the \(b\) direction, a diameter of \(7.3\,\mathrm{\AA}\) of the channel in Co-btz-ht was observed, whereas Ni-btz-ht had a diameter of \(8.7\,\mathrm{\AA}\) (Fig. S2 in the ESM). On the basis of little difference in the initial channel sizes, Co-btz experienced more deformation and was more flexible than Ni-btz. The structural flexibility of resulted MOFs was closely bound up with the metal ions in the frameworks, in accordance with the trends proposed in the Irving-Williams series [35]. Furthermore, the reversible transformations were studied by immersing the samples with narrow channels in DMSO for \(10\,\mathrm{h}\). Comparison of PXRD patterns of these resulted samples (named as Co-btz-ht-DMSO and Ni-btz-ht-DMSO, respectively) with the simulated PXRD patterns as well as that of as-synthesized samples (Fig. S3 in the ESM) indicates that these heated phases transformed back to their as-synthesized phases respectively and the long-range periodic order was not affected by their flexibility. These observations revealed that the structural integrity and substantial changes were consistent with "breathing effect" of flexible MOFs which were induced by the interactions between host frameworks and guest solvents [29]. Subsequently,
the solvent-exchanged phases of Co-btz and Ni-btz (denoted as Co-btz-m and Ni-btz-m, respectively) were studied to confirm their flexible frameworks. As shown in Fig. 2, the PXRD patterns of resulted MOFs as well as their partial and fully activation phases were collected to further probe the effects of solvents and activation methods on MOFs' flexibility. For both MOFs soaking in methanol, the (101) peaks exhibited small displacement indicating the solvent-induced flexibility. The intensity of (101) peak greatly decreased, and the intensity of peak around \(9.8^{\circ}\) increased for M-btz \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}\), Ni) which might be attributed to new phase with different guest solvents or partial framework decomposition. And then, the solvent-exchanged samples were treated at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 4 and \(24\,\mathrm{h}\), respectively. From the comparison of PXRD patterns, we can see that these activation methods didn't change the main structure of these samples (Fig. 2). To make sure the complete evacuation of the porous frameworks, we used the treatment for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) as the activated samples for further gas sorption measurements. According to PXRD and TGA results (Fig. S1 in the ESM), activated methanol-exchanged samples possessed less-open frameworks, in which \(2\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) guest molecules for Co-btz and \(7\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) guest molecules for Ni-btz were found. As for the ethanol-exchanged samples (Co-btz-e and Ni-btz-e), both of them show the original (101) and (002) peaks shift to higher \(2\theta\) angles, and Co-btz shifts larger displacements than Ni-btz, further confirming the more flexibility of Co-btz. PXRD (Fig. 2) and TGA (Fig. S1 in the ESM) results indicate that their activated samples at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) maintained the less-open frameworks induced by ethanol, and \(5\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) guest molecules for Co-btz and \(3.5\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) guest molecules for Ni-btz were left in the frameworks. As the activation time prolonging to \(24\,\mathrm{h}\), ethanol-soaked samples returned to narrow frameworks which can be identified as their corresponding heated phases. Therefore, the activated ethanol-exchanged samples will represent the samples treated for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\). Furthermore, we investigated the structure changes of Co-btz-e treated at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for periods from 0 to \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). The PXRD patterns (Fig. S5 in the ESM) of these samples presented distinct structural changes via the continuous displacements of characteristic peaks of (101) and (002), indicating that the phase transformation via ethanol exchange is continuous. Compared the phase transformation by methanol with by ethanol, we can see that methanol caused a greater structural change which is probably because of its smaller size and greater polarity.
## 3.3 Stability of Co-btz-ht and Ni-btz-ht
In order to prove that the synthesized cationic azole-based MOFs possess high chemical stability, the PXRD patterns of narrow-pore materials immersing in the acid or alkali aqueous solutions with different pH values of 1–12 for \(20\,\mathrm{h}\) were collected (Fig. S4 in the ESM). It was found that their frameworks were well-retained, suggesting remarkable chemical stability of synthesized MOFs, which was consistent with the results previously reported [14, 16]. Interestingly, narrow pore materials of M-btz-ht \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}\), Ni) placed at room temperature for 18 months exhibited open framework structures, proving the flexible features have been successfully introduced into the cationic azole-based MOFs and these flexible MOFs have high stability over 1 year.
In order to further prove the stability of resulted MOFs, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption experiments of the treated materials were performed at \(273\,\mathrm{K}\), and the results were compared with the adsorption isotherms of the directly-activated materials. As indicated in Fig. S5 in the ESM, treated Co-btz-ht samples, which were immersed in HCl solution \(\mathrm{(pH}=1)\) and KOH solution \(\mathrm{(pH}=12)\) for \(20\,\mathrm{h}\), exhibited similar \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption
isotherms compared with the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms of directly-activated Co-btz-ht. Ni-btz-ht treated in acidic condition possessed similar \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption uptakes with Ni-btz-ht, whereas Ni-btz-ht treated in basic condition showed a little decrease. Combining with the PXRD patterns of treated samples compared to as-synthesized MOFs discussed above, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms of treated samples demonstrated the good stabilities of resulted MOFs.
## 3.4 Permanent porosity of different phases
To explore the porosity of activated MOFs as well as the effects of different activation methods on MOFs' flexibility, \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption isotherms for different phases of both MOFs were measured. The heated phases of both MOFs possessed negligible \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) uptakes at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) (Fig. 3), which may be caused by unsuitable activations, interaction between the framework and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) molecules or slow diffusion [24]. Since the kinetic diameter of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is smaller than that of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) sorption isotherms at \(195\,\mathrm{K}\) of Co-btz-ht and Ni-btz-ht were collected to identify their pore structures. As present in Fig. 3, a large hysteresis loop was observed in the isotherms of Co-btz-ht due to the framework stretch, whereas Ni-btz-ht exhibited a representative type-I isotherm corresponding to a microporous framework, which was consistent with the simulated model. According to the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) model, surface areas calculated from \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) sorption isotherms were \(112\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) for Co-btz-ht and \(354\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) for Ni-btz-ht, respectively.
We also measured \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption of activated Co-btz-m, Co-btz-e, Ni-btz-m and Ni-btz-e at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\). As shown in Fig. 4, the isotherms of Co-btz-e, Ni-btz-m and Ni-btz-e presented sharp \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) uptakes at low relative pressures and obvious hysteresis loops in certain relative pressure ranges, reflecting their micro-mesoporous textures. This was further proved by their pore size distributions (Figs. S7 and S8 in the ESM) calculated by non-local density functional theory (NLDFT). The existence of mesopores might be attributed to partial structure collapses caused by the solvents which is not rare for other MOFs [38]. These isotherms also suggested that no structure transformations occurred for these phases during \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\). What is different, the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption isotherm of activated Co-btz-m at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) exhibited a stepped profile and a large hysteresis loop, which suggested structure transformation of Co-btz-m occurred during \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption process. \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm for activated Co-btz-m was measured at \(195\,\mathrm{K}\) to further verify the dynamic changes of the framework. As shown in Figs. 4(c) and 4(d), the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) sorption isotherm of Co-btz-m showed a gate-opening step with an onset relative pressure of ca. 0.01, followed by a gradual increase in uptakes which reached up to \(253\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) (STP) at 1 atm. The BET surface areas calculated from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) for different phases of both MOFs varied from 14 to \(1,010\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) (Table S3 in the ESM), and activated ethanol-exchanged samples possessed the highest surface areas for both MOFs.
