## 3.5 Adsorption and separation properties
Based on the successful preparation of various phases, we evaluated the gas adsorption and separation of these samples due to their differentiated pore textures. Single component \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) adsorption isotherms were collected for each activated sample at 273 and \(298\,\mathrm{K}\), respectively. As illustrated in Figs. 5(a)–5(d), all the samples exhibited higher \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptakes than \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) in all the pressure range suggesting their potential separation abilities. Because of the highest surface areas, activated Ni-btz-e possessed the highest uptakes for both \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\). The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity at 1 atm was \(48.3\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) (273 K, STP) and \(40.6\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) (298 K, STP) for activated Co-btz-m, which was only slightly higher than that of Co-btz-ht (\(43.7\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) at \(273\,\mathrm{K}\), \(27.8\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) at \(298\,\mathrm{K}\)), whereas Ni-btz-ht presented higher capacity (\(94.9\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) at \(273\,\mathrm{K}\), \(55.0\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) at \(298\,\mathrm{K}\)) than activated Ni-btz-m (\(68.3\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) at \(273\,\mathrm{K}\), \(34.6\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\) at \(298\,\mathrm{K}\)) under the same test conditions.
To acquire further insight into the affinity of MOF host to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), the isosteric heats of adsorption (\(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}\)) were calculated according to the Clausius–Clapeyron equation [39]. At zero loading, the \(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}\) values (Fig. S9 in the ESM) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) for the heated phases were higher than other MOF phases suggesting stronger interactions with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules, which probably benefited from their narrow rhombus channels. Notably, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) \(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}\) of Co-btz-ht reached up to \(38\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}\), comparable to some \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)-philic MOFs [40]. Because of the less flexibility of Ni-btz than Co-btz, the \(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}\) values of Ni-btz showed less difference than that of Co-btz. All the solvent-exchanged samples exhibited comparable \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) \(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}\) with most MOFs such as HKUST-I (\(31\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}\)) [41], SHF-61-DMF (\(22\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}\)) [29] and ZU-66 (\(31\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}\)) [42]. The high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) \(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}\) could be attributed to the presence of the uncoordinated N-heteroatom sites in the frameworks as well as the cationic framework [14, 40].
The ideal solution adsorbed theory (IAST) was used to assess the selectivity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) for different phases. The dual-site
Langmuir-Freundlich (DSLF) equation was selected to fit the experimental single component isotherms at first. It is resulted that all the isotherms of both \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) at \(273~\mathrm{K}\) fitted very well (Fig. S10 and Table S2 in the ESM). The selectivity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) (50:50 in volume) was 63 for Co-btz-ht, while the selectivity was 54 for Ni-btz-ht at \(273~\mathrm{K}\) and 1 atm (Figs. 5(e) and 5(f)). However, other activated phases exhibited lower selectivity in spite of higher surface areas, indicating MOFs' structural features play a more important role in the selectivity of gas mixture. Among MOFs with high selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\), Co-btz-ht and Ni-btz-ht also represented comparable IAST selectivity under similar conditions (Table S4 in the ESM).
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, a linear azole-based ligand was selected and two isomorphic cationic MOFs (M-btz-as, \(\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Co}\), Ni) with different flexibility were synthesized. Co-btz-as possessed more flexible framework than Ni-btz-as identified by the comparison of PXRD patterns of M-btz-ht phases. Because of the structural flexibility of MOFs was closely bound up with the metal ions in the frameworks, which was in accordance with the trends proposed in the Irving-Williams series. Interestingly, activated M-btz-m and activated M-btz-e were obtained by different solvent-mediated activation methods benefiting from the special host–guest interaction of flexible MOFs and continuous breathing was proved by PXRD. It is noteworthy that different MOFs phases exhibited different gas separation abilities. Especially, Co-btz-ht with relatively narrow channel structure exhibited the highest \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) separation ratio of 63, which is higher than most reported rigid structure MOFs materials. This work provided a clear insight into detailed structural investigations within a flexible MOFs via diverse activation methods, and will open a new avenue in designing flexible ionic azole-based MOFs for gas separation.
